From def74a403f0d3ca40463096a2730c2e4edf4da6e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ender Hsieh <andhsieh@nvidia.com>
Date: Mon, 29 Jun 2026 14:23:40 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] nvidia-gpu: recover from MCTP endpoint removal

mctpd takes an endpoint object away when recovery fails within Treclaim,
or when the device is reset or power cycled. Nothing here noticed: the
device kept polling an endpoint that no longer existed, and it never
resumed once the endpoint came back.

Watch mctpd for the endpoint going away and returning, and give the
device an Offline state to sit in while it is gone. Its D-Bus objects
are kept throughout, so a consumer sees the same objects go unavailable
rather than objects that disappear and reappear.

An endpoint that comes back need not come back at the same path: the
path carries the EID, and the EID can change. Match it by the endpoint's
UUID instead and re-bind the existing device to the new EID in place,
rather than rebuilding it and taking away the objects a consumer is
already watching.

The SMA also gains Inventory.Item, OperationalStatus and a contained_by
association to the object its configuration sits under, so it is
parented on the board rather than floating at the inventory root.

Scope is SMA only; bridged GPUs behind the SMA cannot be recovered by
mctpd's .Recover yet (upstream limitation).

Tested: Build an image for nvl32-obmc machine with the following
patches cherry-picked:

```
1. Align with upstream u-boot dts tree:
   https://gerrit.openbmc.org/c/openbmc/openbmc/+/89932
2. mctpd configuration:
   https://gerrit.openbmc.org/c/openbmc/openbmc/+/87390
3. Enable nvidia-gpu sensor:
   https://gerrit.openbmc.org/c/openbmc/openbmc/+/89933

Each SMA now exposes an inventory object carrying OperationalStatus,
parented on the board its configuration was resolved against:

  # busctl introspect xyz.openbmc_project.GpuSensor \
        /xyz/openbmc_project/inventory/GPU_SMA_0
    xyz.openbmc_project.Association.Definitions
    .Associations  a(sss) 1 "contained_by" "containing"
      ".../inventory/system/board/Nvidia_IMGX_ConnectX8_SuperNIC_Switch"
    xyz.openbmc_project.Inventory.Item
    xyz.openbmc_project.State.Decorator.OperationalStatus
    .Functional    b true

Held an SMA in reset over its I/O expander so mctpd would take the
endpoint object away, rather than recovering it in milliseconds:

  # gpioset --mode=time --sec=1 gpiochip4 4=0

  endpoints                23 -> 21 -> 17
  inventory/GPU_SMA_0      Functional=false   (GPU_SMA_1 stayed true)
  sensor GPU_SMA_0_TEMP_0  Functional=false, held for over 60s

The device stayed Offline with its D-Bus objects intact rather than
disappearing. Restoring the SMA brought all eight back to Functional
true, 62 sensors, service active with no restarts.
```
1. Verified removal -> Offline and restore -> re-attach via the
   nvidiagpusensor journal and busctl on the BMC.
2. Verified the D-Bus objects and device names are preserved across
   the outage.

[1] https://gerrit.openbmc.org/c/openbmc/dbus-sensors/+/91802/25

